= Justification — Technical =

== Propositional / doxastic distinction ==
Let E be a subject's available evidence.

'''Propositional justification''' concerns whether E sufficiently supports p.

'''Doxastic justification''' additionally requires that the actual belief B(p) be appropriately based on the supporting grounds.

This blocks cases where the subject possesses good evidence but believes p from wishful thinking or an unrelated inference.

== Basing theories ==
Causal theories require the reason to appropriately cause the belief.

Counterfactual theories ask whether changes in the reason would appropriately change the belief.

Doxastic accounts require a meta-level recognition that the reason supports p.

Dispositional accounts appeal to patterns of responsiveness to reasons.

All face versions of deviant-causal-chain or over-intellectualization problems.

== Internalist families ==
Access internalism requires relevant justifiers to be reflectively accessible.

Mentalism constrains justification to supervene on mental states.

Evidential internalism connects permissible belief to evidence possessed by the subject.

These positions are not identical.

== Externalist families ==
Process reliabilism evaluates the truth ratio/reliability of the belief-forming process.

Virtue reliabilism attributes knowledge/justification to successful exercise of epistemic competence.

Proper-function theories relate warrant to cognitive systems functioning as designed/appropriate in suitable environments.

== Structure ==
Foundationalism terminates epistemic dependence in basic justification.

Coherentism distributes justification across a mutually supporting belief system.

Infinitism permits infinite non-circular chains of reasons.

Hybrid views combine experiential foundations with coherence constraints.

== Defeat ==
A defeasible justification relation can be represented schematically:

<pre>
E supports p
D undercuts(E → p)
therefore support(E,p) is reduced or defeated
</pre>

Higher-order evidence can itself be a defeater when it concerns the reliability or rationality of one's own first-order reasoning.

